l calls, closures, continuations, dynamic state, result/audit records, capabilities, and host handles, and keep raw host objects outside Scheme-visible roots.\n- The portable/compiled heap bridge ([#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662)) should follow the pre-1.0 portable owned value/identity cluster ([#346](/tahoma/consent/issues/346)-[#350](/tahoma/consent/issues/350)) plus the baseline compiled heap and root path ([#120](/tahoma/consent/issues/120), [#333](/tahoma/consent/issues/333), [#334](/tahoma/consent/issues/334), [#335](/tahoma/consent/issues/335), and [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336)), and should prevent interpreted portable code and compiled code from growing divergent object identity, mutation, traversal, host-handle, or diagnostic models.\n- Compiled heap and GC stress diagnostics ([#663](/tahoma/consent/issues/663)) should follow [#333](/tahoma/consent/issues/333)-[#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336) and the bridge [#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662), then provide deterministic forced-collection fixtures, leak/root-retention checks, and Scheme-readable heap/root/profiling records before the metacircular compiled root exercises long-running compiled heap behavior.\n- LLVM textual IR emission ([#119](/tahoma/consent/issues/119)) should follow [#115](/tahoma/consent/issues/115), [#116](/tahoma/consent/issues/116), [#117](/tahoma/consent/issues/117), [#118](/tahoma/consent/issues/118), [#120](/tahoma/consent/issues/120), and [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336), and must not introduce Go tooling.\n- Precise native root metadata ([#664](/tahoma/consent/issues/664)) should follow [#119](/tahoma/consent/issues/119), [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336), and [#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662), extending the baseline shadow-stack/root-stack path with stack-map, register-root, and safepoint metadata for optimized native code without blocking the first pure LLVM textual emitter.\n- Compiled effects ([#121](/tahoma/consent/issues/121)) should follow ~~[#98](/tahoma/consent/issues/98)~~, ~~[#102](/tahoma/consent/issues/102)~~, ~~[#103](/tahoma/consent/issues/103)~~, [#115](/tahoma/consent/issues/115), [#120](/tahoma/consent/issues/120), and [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336) so native backends share the same policy-gated effect path as the interpreter without losing policy-visible records across collection points.\n- The self-hosted collector/runtime transition ([#667](/tahoma/consent/issues/667)) should follow [#119](/tahoma/consent/issues/119), [#121](/tahoma/consent/issues/121), [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336), and [#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662), and should define the irreducible allocation/collection substrate versus collector/runtime support that can be authored as portable Consent Scheme without circular bootstrap failure.\n- Consent-owned weak references, finalizers, and ephemerons ([#666](/tahoma/consent/issues/666)) should follow [#335](/tahoma/consent/issues/335), [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336), [#581](/tahoma/consent/issues/581), [#661](/tahoma/consent/issues/661), and [#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662), replacing the host-backed ephemeron substrate with collector-owned semantics only after the compiled heap exists.\n- Moving and compacting collector support ([#665](/tahoma/consent/issues/665)) should follow [#335](/tahoma/consent/issues/335), [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336), [#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662), and [#664](/tahoma/consent/issues/664), so relocation can update both baseline and optimized roots without exposing raw host objects.\n- Generational GC and write barriers ([#668](/tahoma/consent/issues/668)) should follow [#335](/tahoma/consent/issues/335), [#336](/tahoma/consent/issues/336), [#662](/tahoma/consent/issues/662), [#664](/tahoma/consent/issues/664), and [#665](/tahoma/consent/issues/665), adding mutation barriers and remembered-set/card-table diagnostics after movement and precise roots are available.\n- The Emacs Lisp byte-code backend contract ([#123](/tahoma/consent/issues/123)) should