15), [#128](/tahoma/consent/issues/128), ~~[#300](/tahoma/consent/issues/300)~~, and ~~[#303](/tahoma/consent/issues/303)~~).\n- The language-front-end tooling family (umbrella [#503](/tahoma/consent/issues/503), tools [#494](/tahoma/consent/issues/494)–[#502](/tahoma/consent/issues/502)) consumes the reader-tooling substrate and the `(agent diagnostics)` contract rather than re-parsing source. Substrate-readiness gates promotion order: the macro stepper ([#496](/tahoma/consent/issues/496)), the validator ([#497](/tahoma/consent/issues/497), on shipped recovery ~~[#418](/tahoma/consent/issues/418)~~), the xref index ([#498](/tahoma/consent/issues/498)), the dependency analyzer ([#499](/tahoma/consent/issues/499)), and the doctest runner ([#500](/tahoma/consent/issues/500)) have no open substrate blocker and are the near-term tier; the formatter ([#494](/tahoma/consent/issues/494)) and the rewrite engine's comment-preserving path ([#495](/tahoma/consent/issues/495)) follow the CST view ([#417](/tahoma/consent/issues/417)); the highlighter ([#502](/tahoma/consent/issues/502)) follows the comment tokens ([#416](/tahoma/consent/issues/416)); and the language server ([#501](/tahoma/consent/issues/501)) splits into a tool half and a host half — its language-intelligence library (the tool) follows [#417](/tahoma/consent/issues/417) and the xref index ([#498](/tahoma/consent/issues/498)), while the long-running server is the editor host (the LSP/DAP adapter [#147](/tahoma/consent/issues/147), Chunk H) that serves that library, so the dependency flows host→tool and [#501](/tahoma/consent/issues/501) carries no blocked_by edge to [#147](/tahoma/consent/issues/147). The family shares this substrate with the lint umbrella ([#419](/tahoma/consent/issues/419)/[#420](/tahoma/consent/issues/420)) and docgen ([#493](/tahoma/consent/issues/493)), so [#416](/tahoma/consent/issues/416)/[#417](/tahoma/consent/issues/417) changes should be weighed against the whole consumer set; like Chunk O generally, none of [#494](/tahoma/consent/issues/494)–[#503](/tahoma/consent/issues/503) is an implementation prerequisite for spine work until promoted.\n- Compiled and reference runtime documentation metadata ([#304](/tahoma/consent/issues/304)) should follow rich documentation records (~~[#303](/tahoma/consent/issues/303)~~), the R7RS and custom library reference work ([#24](/tahoma/consent/issues/24) and [#284](/tahoma/consent/issues/284)), and the LLIR front door ([#115](/tahoma/consent/issues/115), [#116](/tahoma/consent/issues/116), and [#120](/tahoma/consent/issues/120)) so interpreter and compiled-runtime reflection share one Scheme-readable documentation record shape. Case-lambda clause docstring metadata ([#340](/tahoma/consent/issues/340)) should follow ~~[#303](/tahoma/consent/issues/303)~~ and [#304](/tahoma/consent/issues/304) because it needs clause-level procedure metadata rather than the initial single-string procedure docstring slot.\n- The docstring-driven reference generator ([#493](/tahoma/consent/issues/493)) should follow the already-shipped documentation records (~~[#301](/tahoma/consent/issues/301)~~, ~~[#302](/tahoma/consent/issues/302)~~, ~~[#303](/tahoma/consent/issues/303)~~, and ~~[#344](/tahoma/consent/issues/344)~~) and the in-language documentation pipeline ([#454](/tahoma/consent/issues/454) reader plus the [#455](/tahoma/consent/issues/455)/[#456](/tahoma/consent/issues/456) emitters), is placed in the pre-1.0 documentation chunk (0.34) so 1.0 ships a self-generated library and top-level reference, targets the interpreted runtime, and feeds — rather than duplicates — the curated custom library reference docs ([#284](/tahoma/consent/issues/284)); compiled-runtime documentation parity for the generated reference is deferred to [#304](/tahoma/consent/issues/304) (post-1.0, Chunk 1.2) so it is explicitly not a 1.0 blocker.\n- Typed docstring metadata (~~[#604](/tahoma/consent/issues/604)~~) should follow the shipped rich metadata